  "live_records": {
    "q-124234": {
      "dom_id": "step-q124234",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14216490/assets/43c8d1ced440e0fe79052c1329ff3af865e1fdbfb4cc0c7ad0a9c176592358e9.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(-1,1)$ to the point $(2,2),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨3t-1,\\,t+1⟩.$*",
      "worked_solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(-1,1)$ and $(2,2).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(-1+t(2-(-1))),\\,(1+t(2-1))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3t-1,\\,t+1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-1)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3t-1)+(t+1)^{2} \\\\  & =(3t-1)+({t}^{2}+2t+1) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+5t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+5t)\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+5t)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
