    "q-124246": {
      "dom_id": "step-q124246",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14216490/assets/88da0f1ddf1f605186c9d921a9eade24b5f0aa9de2ae4afdc51a921586093b16.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(-2,3)$ to the point $(0,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨2t-2,\\,3-3t⟩.$*",
      "worked_solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(-2,3)$ and $(0,0).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(-2+t(0-(-2))),\\,(3+t(0-3))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t-2,\\,3-3t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-2)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2t-2)^{2}(3-3t) \\\\  & =4(t-1)^{2}\\cdot 3(1-t) \\\\  & =-12(t-1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-12(t-1)^{3}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-24{\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
