    "q-124172": {
      "dom_id": "step-q124172",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14216490/assets/09a38bb4306728f97b7fecbf9ab1bda7aa957607352c1ab27af09489c271c3ed.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}({y}^{2}-2x)\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path traversed along the line segment from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(2,3),$ as shown above.",
      "worked_solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(0,1)$ and $(2,3).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(0+t(2-0)),\\,(1+t(3-1))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,1+2t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+2t)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-2x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(1+2t)^{2}-2(2t) \\\\  & =1+4t+4{t}^{2}-4t \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}+t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{4}{3}+1)-2(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
