    "q-124212": {
      "dom_id": "step-q124212",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14216490/assets/dfa95100566fce9ab113b00ae53d1a76e8977b68aa4bac0e9b6892a9930b70d4.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{y{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}}{8x}\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along the lower half of the circle of radius $4,$ traversed from the point $(-4,0)$ to the point $(4,0),$ as shown above.",
      "worked_solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $4$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{y{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}}{8x},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{3}}{8(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\operatorname{sin}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}{32\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-8[\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ] \\\\  & =-8(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
